+    "title": "Session protocol: SessionMsg, SessionEndpoint and ReplayRing",
+    "description": "Sans-IO session protocol (R3, R4): message codec, per-direction offsets with exactly-once dedup, acks, 2 s heartbeats and the 8 s silence verdict, resume negotiation, and the replay ring.\nSpec: docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-24-mosh-phase1-resumable-session/2026-10-24-mosh-phase1-resumable-session-design.md (§Session wire protocol, §Ring caps and drain policy, §Key abstractions SessionMsg/SessionEndpoint/ReplayRing).\nPure, sans-IO implementation in src/mosh/session.rs and src/mosh/ring.rs: the six messages [u8 type][u32 LE len][payload] with incremental decoder; HELLO/WELCOME/END JSON bodies; 128-bit Token from /dev/urandom; per-direction absolute byte offsets, dedup of overlap, gap = leg protocol error; ack every 64 KiB or 200 ms and on heartbeat; 2 s heartbeat, 8 s silence verdict with time passed in; resume replay from peer recv_offset; ReplayRing bounded by caller-supplied capacity with push/ack-trim/replay_from/free-space and overflow reported.\nowns: SessionMsg; owns: SessionEndpoint; owns: ReplayRing\nconsumes: mosh constants and MoshConfig\nconsumes: mosh module tree\nblocked-by mosh-dqw.1: consumes mosh module tree\nFiles: src/mosh/session.rs, src/mosh/ring.rs\nAcceptance:\n- [ ] Unit tests: loss at every offset of a scripted transcript then resume delivers exactly-once in order both directions; duplicate and overlapping DATA discarded; gap rejected as leg error; double reconnect; END both directions; unknown/ended welcome surfaces resume-refused; ack cadence; 8 s silence verdict from injected clock.\n- [ ] Idle test (R17): two endpoints exchanging only heartbeats for \u003e8 s of injected time never reach the silence verdict; heartbeats are timer-driven, not piggybacked on data.\n- [ ] Heartbeat interval, silence timeout and ring caps are compile-time constants; grace is carried in HELLO.grace_ms (R18).\n- [ ] Ring tests: wraparound, ack-trim, replay boundaries, overflow reported never silent.\n- [ ] No sockets, threads or system clock used in these files.",
